{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210692,"nonce":"4AceG2GLl/QADT6H52+I9QpSKus=","title":"No OSR into JIT for a hot caller: method_call and delta_blue slower than reference Wren","message":"Tiered mode (Cranelift, 2026-04-26, 5 runs): fib 21x faster than stock Wren, binary_trees at parity, method_call 1.2x slower, delta_blue 2.1x slower.\n\nThe hot loop lives in a function that runs once (`\u003cmodule\u003e` or a top-level driver) so it is never entered through the JIT'd version. Interpreter IC fast path costs ~75 ns per call (decode + FFI + class check) vs ~10 ns for JIT pure_leaf and ~10 ns for Wren's C interpreter.\n\nAlready in place: speculative devirtualisation from IC snapshots, CallKnownFunc with expected_class / inline_getter_field / pure_leaf, trivial getter inlining, wren_known_call_N, eager callee queuing, back-edge compile drain, back-edge OSR entries for loops.\n\nBlocked on: true mid-function OSR for the running frame (restart-from-top re-executes side effects), an IC table that is enriched between baseline and optimised tiers (currently identical coverage), and no computed-goto dispatch in Rust for the interpreter itself.","files":null}]}